---
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { pickAndPlaceSource, pickAndPlaceValidate } from '../data/examples';
import { CORE_REPO, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="RoboLang: a task language for robots and AI"
  description="RoboLang is an open-source task description language for robots. Write tasks, validate their structure and inspect a versioned intermediate representation."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <ul class="eyebrow" aria-label="Project facts">
        <li>Open source</li>
        <li>Developer preview</li>
        <li>Language {versions.language}</li>
      </ul>
      <h1>A task language for robots and AI.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Write robotic tasks. Validate their structure. Inspect a versioned intermediate
        representation.
      </p>
      <p>
        RoboLang describes <em>what</em> a robot should do in a small, typed, human-readable text
        format. Today it ships read-only developer tools that check a task file and show exactly what
        it says, before anything is connected to a robot.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={CORE_REPO}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <CodeBlock code={pickAndPlaceSource} label="examples/pick_and_place.rob" />
      <CodeBlock code={pickAndPlaceValidate} label="robolang validate examples/pick_and_place.rob" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Robot tasks are often written as ad hoc scripts or free-form text. A typo, a missing argument or
      a name that was never declared is found late, usually when something is already running. That
      gets worse when the task text is produced by a language model instead of a person.
    </p>
    <p>
      RoboLang's purpose is to give a task a precise written form with a published grammar, so that
      tools can reject a malformed task with a clear message and hand a well-formed one to other
      software as structured data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What works today</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Distribution {versions.distribution} installs one command, <code>robolang</code>, with two
      read-only subcommands. It needs Python {versions.pythonMinimum} or newer and has no other
      dependencies.
    </p>
    <ul class="cards">
      <li class="card">
        <h3>Write</h3>
        <p>
          Language {versions.language} has a formal grammar: tasks with typed parameters, <code
            >@safety</code
          > annotations, a <code>pre</code> block and a <code>plan</code> of six statement kinds.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/reference/">Language reference</a></p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3>Validate</h3>
        <p>
          <code>robolang validate</code> checks syntax, names, types and values, and reports each
          problem as <code>file:line:column</code> with a stable diagnostic code.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/examples/#invalid">See diagnostics</a></p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3>Inspect</h3>
        <p>
          <code>robolang inspect</code> shows what a valid file describes, as text or as JSON in IR
          schema {versions.irSchema}, for use by other tools.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/examples/#json-ir">See the JSON IR</a></p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="limitations">What it does not do</h2>
    <p>These limits are deliberate for the developer preview. Please read them before you start.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>It does not execute tasks.</strong> The supported package contains no code that can run
        a task or move a robot, and there is no command that does.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It does not enforce safety.</strong>
        <code>@safety</code> limits and <code>pre</code> conditions are parsed and checked as text. They
        are not evaluated or enforced by anything. A file that validates is well formed; it is not
        thereby safe to run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It has no ROS 2 integration.</strong> The core repository keeps an early, never
        validated ROS 2 adapter as historical source. It is not installed and not supported.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It does not generate tasks with AI.</strong> The language is designed to be easy for
        language models to write, but no model or planner is included.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It is an alpha.</strong> It is installed from a clone of the repository and is not
        published on PyPI. Details may change between versions.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Execution, interoperability and AI-assisted authoring are research directions, described on the
      <a href="/roadmap/">Vision &amp; Roadmap</a> page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      Robotics developers, ROS 2 engineers and researchers who want to experiment with a checkable
      task format, including as a target for tasks written by language models. Feedback on the
      language and the tools is the main thing the project needs right now.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/get-started/">Install and try it</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/contribute/">Give feedback</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
